WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:00.960
Vsauce!

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.920
Merlin here -- and I know everything about
what you know and think.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.600
Everything.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:13.040
I’ve built a vacation home in your mind
and I’m lounging by the pool sipping chocolate

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.920
milk right now.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.800
And I can prove it with a little magic that
amazed TV audiences worldwide by having them

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.640
rub their fingers all over their screens.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:32.720
It starts with a clock, and each of the 12
numbers are arranged as you’d expect.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.720
You’ll be moving around this clock in a
way that’s random to me -- you’ll know

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.280
which numbers you choose along the way, but
I won’t.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:43.840
How could I?

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.080
You’re wherever you are, and I’m in my
mom’s basement.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:51.520
Right?

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.020
Wrong!

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.560
Well, I am in my mom's basement.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:01.200
But with the power of my fake wizard beard,
no matter what number you choose I will know

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.480
where you are.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.880
Now clear your mind of its usual thoughts
-- memes, pizza rolls, energy drinks -- and

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.080
select a number.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:20.320
It can be any number from 1 to 12, and only
you will know what it is.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.120
Ready?

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:21.620
Go.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:22.640
Pick a number.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.360
Got a number?

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.080
Good.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:34.480
Now I need you to spell your number, starting
at 12 and moving clockwise one step for each

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.160
letter in the word.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:47.760
If your secret number is 7, then you’d move
5 steps -- s-e-v-e-n -- and land on the 5.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.800
If you picked 11, you’d move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:57.520
6 steps -- e-l-e-v-e-n
-- and land on the 6.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:04.560
Hold your finger over the screen starting
at 12 and count your number’s letters until

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.720
you’re on your new number.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:07.280
Okay?

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:07.840
Go ahead.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:15.440
Alright.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.840
Now spell your new number, moving one position
clockwise for each letter in that number.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:27.920
If you’re on the 3, then&nbsp;
move 5 spaces -- t-h-r-e-e.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.400
If you’re on the 6, then move 3 spaces -- s-i-x.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:44.000
If you run out of spaces and get back to 12,
just keep going around the same way a clock’s

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:44.960
hands move.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.160
Okay.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:50.800
Did you do it?

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:58.400
We're gonna do this one more time -- spell
your new number again and stop.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.840
I’ll give ya a second to do that.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:06.180
Okay.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:14.240
I have no idea what number you started with
or the second one you landed on, let alone

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:21.280
the third number that you landed on after
that -- but I do know that you’re not on

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.880
the 2, so I’m gonna get rid of that.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.920
And I know that you’re not on the 4, either.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.840
Or the 8.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.040
Or the 12.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.360
As I sip my delicious chocolate milk next
to your mind pool I can see that you weren’t

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.800
on any of those numbers so we don’t need
them.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:45.840
Hey.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.480
Let’s go one more round.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.760
Spell your new number, and skip the empty
spaces.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.120
One letter, one position, and stop when you’re
done.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:08.880
If you’re on the 5, you’ll move 4 spaces
-- f-i-v-e -- skipping the empty 8 space and

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.320
landing on the 10.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.840
Go ahead.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:18.480
Are you on the 10?

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:19.940
No.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:20.900
No!

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:21.760
No.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.080
You are not on the 10.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.080
So the 10 is gone.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.400
You're on the 3?

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.640
No, no, no.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.560
You are not on the 3.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.000
That's gone too.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:34.640
The 9.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:35.940
Gone.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:37.360
Or the 1.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:38.580
Gone.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:49.280
You’re on the 6 -- and you've just witnessed
the magic of The Kruskal Count.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.320
Alright hold on this beard is really getting
itchy.This hat is super tight.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.960
Hang on.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.640
Oh the troubles of having a beard.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:02.880
That’s better.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.000
So magician David Copperfield has performed
illusions with several different variations

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.720
of this trick, from using a clock face to
celebrities to vacation destinations.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:21.760
He mesmerized audiences in live shows and
TV shows with his predictive power.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.600
But the man who discovered The Kruskal Count
was mathematician and physicist Martin Kruskal

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:34.080
-- who took some delight in knowing that it
often confused actual magicians because it

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.600
wasn’t based on any kind of sleight of hand.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.480
It was based on math.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:43.840
So what is actually going here?

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:47.040
Let’s dissect what happens in the clock
trick.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:54.640
You’re starting at 12, and you have four
possible moves: numbers that contain 3, 4,

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.520
5, or 6 letters.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:03.120
We’ve got 12 numbers to choose from, but
we only have 4 possible pathways forward,

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:10.640
and therefore I know no matter what number
you choose, you’ll end up on the 3, 4, 5,

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.880
or 6 after your first move.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:20.240
For your second move, you’ll either move
5, 4, 4, or 3 -- which means that every single

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.000
player winds up on either the 8 or the 9.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:31.760
Notice that at no point in the game can anyone
even land on the 2, 7, 10, 11, 12.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.320
I know that before we start, but you probably
won’t notice it as you play.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:42.400
And along the way, I remind you that these
choices are yours and are totally random to

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:50.000
me, because reinforcing that randomness distracts
you from thinking about any pattern that you

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.840
might be encountering.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.720
On the third move, if you’re on the 8, you’ll
move 5 spaces to the 1.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.760
If you’re on the 9, you’ll move 4 spaces
to the 1.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:06.320
And that’s it -- it’s already game over
for you, and you didn’t even know it.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.040
Then the performance really starts.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:15.360
I can take away numbers like 2, 4, 8, and
12 because I know for a fact that you’re

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.560
on the 1.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.800
It’s 100% guaranteed.

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:19.860
But.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:26.880
There’s a very subtle element to choosing
which numbers to erase -- those four removed

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:34.080
numbers have letter values of 3, 4, 5, and
6, so that subconsciously reinforces that

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:40.160
I’m not eliminating some specific element
or pattern, like just removing all of the

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.880
possibilities with 3 letters.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:49.520
It’s spread out among the choices, so to
you it seems just that much more random.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.280
Really, I can do anything once you’re all
on the 1.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.640
I don’t have to remove anything -- if we
just kept playing you’d all just go from

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:58.720
the 1 to the 4.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:05.120
I could also add in as many rounds as I wanted
because you’re all in synch at that point.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.400
Or I could remove different numbers to get
you to the final number that’s best positioned

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:16.800
on camera… which is probably why Copperfield
wanted to land on the 6 in the center of the

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:17.280
screen.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:19.760
It just looked better on TV.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:20.400
So...

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.320
Great so we figured out the trick.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:23.780
Kinda.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.160
Not exactly.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.200
What is happening here?

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:34.160
What Kruskal discovered was that a counting
game like this is really a chain of numbers.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:39.920
Every new number is a new link in the chain,
and when two chains eventually intersect on

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:44.960
the same link, then they become the same chain
from there on out.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:51.920
Knowing that, you can appear to predict a
highly-improbable result even when you don’t

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:54.800
know where the chain began.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:56.880
Let me show you another example.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.960
AThe predictive power of Kruskal’s Count
is seen most easily with a deck of cards.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:08.880
Allow me to show you my Wonderful Weiner Dogs
playing cards.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:15.520
I’ll shuffle a full Wonderful Weiner Dogs
deck of 52 cards and deal out 10.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:20.080
You need to choose one of&nbsp;
these 10 cards -- obviously&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.400
I won’t know which one your choose, because

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:28.640
I recorded this video in the past and you’re
watching it in your present which is my future.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:35.840
Then you’ll proceed to count forward from
that card until we get through the whole deck.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:43.680
Aces count as 1, and face cards count as 5,
and every other card counts as the numerical

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.040
value it displays.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:48.160
So like 9 equals 9.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:52.720
So if you chose to start with card number
5, that's a Queen which is worth 5.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.640
And you’ll go 5 spaces.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:55.140
1,2,3,4,5.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.480
And land on an Ace which is 1.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.680
So then you'll go 1, land on a King which
is 5.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:04.180
1,2,3,4,5.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.280
Land on a 10 and then you'll go 10.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:10.400
Got it?

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:13.920
And you’ll continue this until you can’t
make a full move.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:21.680
So if you land on this 9 you obviously can't
go 9 spaces so this 9 would be your final

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:22.160
card.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:28.640
And even though I don’t know what card you
picked to start, I’ll be able to tell you

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:32.160
which card is the last one in your sequence.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:33.200
Here’s how.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:36.480
I’ll also choose a number from 1-10.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:38.240
Let’s say…

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:39.920
I pick 4.

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.040
Which is a 4.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:47.200
The 4th card will be my first link in the
chain, which Kruskal called a “key card.”

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:52.160
And together, without telling each other,
because I don't know which card you're starting

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:58.240
with, we’re both going to count forward
from our respective cards -- since I’m starting

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:03.680
on the 4th card, which is a 4, I’ll count
4 spaces to another card.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:04.180
1,2,3,4.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:09.840
Which is a 5, and continue until the end.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.240
You’ll do the same thing from your mystery
card.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:13.840
And then uh...

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.440
It’s weiner time.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:15.920
Okay.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:16.640
Go ahead.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:27.840
Let's start!.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:53.440
Your last card in this sequence is that 9
-- because so is mine.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:59.120
Using the Kruskal Count, I can predict what
your final card will be without knowing where

00:11:59.120 --> 00:11:59.920
you started.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:06.800
Because it doesn’t really matter, at a certain
point our chains intersected on the same key

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.720
card and followed the same path to the end.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:13.680
And this always works, doesn’t it?

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:15.040
No, it doesn’t!

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:19.680
About 1 in 6 of you are&nbsp;
already typing a rage-soaked&nbsp;

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:23.200
comment about how dumb I am for choosing the

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:24.320
wrong number.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.240
Joke’s on you, buddy, I knew that would
happen.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:37.600
The Kruskal Count with a 52-card deck only
works about 85% of the time, and if our final

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:43.120
key cards weren’t the same then you chose
one of the numbers that put you on one of

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:48.960
the 15% of chains that land me in the fail
category.

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:56.000
But the longer our chains go, the more chances
they have to intersect -- so if we played

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:03.040
with two decks of 52 cards, the odds of me
predicting the right final card for you jump

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:04.800
to 95%.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:08.720
Let's just see exactly how this can play out.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:14.400
I'll shuffle and deal another 10 cards, color
code them, and mark each chain's key cards.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:20.320
As you can see, 5 out of the 10 cards linked
up right away on this 9 and then ended up

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:21.680
on this 10.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:27.600
The 4th and 9th cards linked up on this 7
and then met up with the others on this Queen.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:33.600
But 3 of the 10 just did their own thing and
converged on a second mini-chain.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:39.840
The 5th, 8th, and 10th cards linked up together
on this Jack and ended up on a 6.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:44.800
There are a couple of reasons for this: first,
landing on a couple of 9s caused huge jumps

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.280
that reduced chain link opportunities -- the&nbsp;

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:50.400
more key cards in a sequence,&nbsp;
the more opportunities

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:52.480
to overlap with the main chain.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:57.040
If we played with two or three decks, our
big chain and little chain would be almost

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:58.480
certain to converge.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:03.920
But this game ended up being a weird outlier
where the magician had a much smaller chance

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:06.480
of success than they usually would.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.400
That’s one way we know that this isn’t
magic.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:17.520
Magic works 100% of the time because it’s
engineered and manufactured to be successful.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:21.680
Probability isn’t quite as predictable.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:26.160
There’s actually a great interactive version
by Alex Frieden and Ravi Montenegro from the

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:31.440
University of Massachusetts Lowell that you
can play online that I’ll link below because

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:32.880
I like you.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:38.800
So yeah this is a fun little trick you can
use to mess with your little brother’s mind,

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.920
but what’s the point?

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:49.600
The Kruskal Count feels like magic, but is
considered a probabilistic paradox -- technically

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:56.640
speaking, it’s a veridical one, and it has
real-world computational applications.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:03.760
In 1978, John Pollard applied it to code-breaking
and developed the A Lambda Method for Catching

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:08.640
Kangaroos also known as the Kangaroo Algorithm.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:12.400
Which is a great name for an algorithm.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:21.200
But to me, the trick is an incredible illustration
of how math can be at the heart of coincidence.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:26.720
What are the chances you run into your old
gym teacher at the supermarket?

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:32.400
What are the odds that you end up going to
the same college as the booger boy who moved

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:34.080
away in first grade?

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:41.360
We’re all living in several different systems
at the same time, some large, some small,

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:51.200
some open, some closed… and every one of
our actions is another link in our own chains.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:58.960
What we call fate or coincidence may really
be the result of a hidden mathematical master

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:06.560
pulling the probability puppet strings of
our existences together -- at which point

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:12.400
we can decide how closely we want to align
our chains from that point on.

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:18.480
Which is cool, because you probably don’t
want to go home with your old gym teacher.

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:25.120
Especially since he saw you buy cat food and
you don’t even have a cat!

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:35.440
And as always -- thanks for watching.

